  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q25-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "25",
    "prompt": "Solve the equation $$(7+4\\sqrt3)^x+(7-4\\sqrt3)^x=4.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=\\pm\\dfrac12$.\n\nSubstitute $t=(7+4\\sqrt3)^x$ and solve $t^2-4t+1=0$.",
      "worked": "Since $(7+4\\sqrt3)(7-4\\sqrt3)=1$, let $t=(7+4\\sqrt3)^x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}t+\\frac1t&=4\\\\t^2+1&=4t\\\\t^2-4t+1&=0\\\\t&=\\frac{4\\pm\\sqrt{16-4}}2\\\\t&=2\\pm\\sqrt3.\\end{align*}$$\nAlso, $7+4\\sqrt3=(2+\\sqrt3)^2$ and $2-\\sqrt3=(2+\\sqrt3)^{-1}$. Thus\n$$\\begin{align*}(2+\\sqrt3)^{2x}&=(2+\\sqrt3)^{\\pm1}\\\\2x&=\\pm1\\\\x&=\\pm\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 86
  },
